  "query_preview": "Forecast Discussion...\n\nOn the hemispheric scale, a strong El Nino pattern persists and will \nlikely continue for the next several months.  Two ridges at 500 mb \nwere noted and about 180 degrees apart.  The first was located over \nthe north Pacific and the other over in Scandinavia. \nStrong/persistent tropical activity over the western Pacific \ncombined with very strong divergence up near 200 mb has led to \nstrong wave-activity fluxes flowing northward from the tropics into \nthe polar regions.  The net result of this will be for intense \nwarming in the stratosphere up near 10mb over the next week leading \nto a sudden stratospheric warming event.  Much of the ensemble data \nagrees with this warming and it will likely perturb the PV and \nweaken it, perhaps splitting it (some solutions) or perhaps simply \nstretching it.  This will likely be one of the earliest (if not the \nearliest) SSW to occur.  The ramifications of this warming event are \nstill unknown at this time.  The main question here is whether there \nwill be a downward reflection from the stratosphere back into the \ntroposphere?  Models pretty much diverge here on that, but I think \nwe'll just have to use the wait and see approach.  The effects of \nthis warming event will not affect our sensible weather in the \nimmediate future.  SSW events often will affect the flow pattern \nsome 3-4 weeks after they occur.  Typically, an SSW can/will weaken \nthe PV and allow higher risks of colder air to work into the eastern \nUS. That could still occur, depending if the PV gets displaced far \nenough south. So we would be looking at late November or early \nDecember to see any effects from this event.\n\nIn the meantime, the strong El Nino background state will continue. \nPressure anomaly maps show a positive east-Asian mountain torque \nalong with an MJO pulse pushing into the west Pacific.  These two \nfeatures will lead to a strengthening and longitudinal extension of \nthe Pacific Jet.  Typically, a +EAMT pattern leads to troughing over \nthe eastern Pacific and western CONUS with subsequent downstream \nridging in the central/eastern US.  Given the El Nino state, the \nsubtropical jet will remain active and strong through the month and \nwill propel numerous disturbances across the CONUS with a risk of \nstrong/severe storms from the southern Plains into the SE US.  Given \nthe strong Pacific flow and forecast high latitude blocking to the \nnorth, intrusions of cold air look to be fairly limited, especially \nwith the Pacific jet flooding the CONUS with mild air.\n\nLocally, we should continue to see normal to above normal \ntemperatures through mid-late November.  Given the expected stronger \njet and multiple systems coming through, precipitation will likely \naverage near normal.\n\nThe overall pattern may shift a bit toward the end of November and \ninto December.  However, this shift is dependent on whether we'll \nsee any downward propagation of warm temps aloft from the \nstratosphere into the troposphere from the early month SSW event. \nWe'll likely see blocking set up with the Euro ensembles showing the \npossible development of an -AO/-NAO/+PNA pattern.  There is a bit \nmore spread on how the Pacific will look as EPO forecasts still have \na bit of spread here.",
